%N A401498 Place n points at the integer polar angles of the Archimedean spiral r = k, 0 <= k < n, then around the k-th point draw a circle whose radius is the distance from the k-th point to the (k+1)-th point: a(n) is the number of vertices in the resulting planar graph.
%C A401498 It is likely all vertices are simple. See the attached image.
%C A401498 See A401497 for further details.
%H A401498 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401498/b401498.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..100</a>
%H A401498 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401498/a401498.jpg">Image for n = 10</a>. The center of the circles are shown as white dots.
%H A401498 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archimedean_spiral">Archimedean spiral</a>.
%F A401498 a(n) = A401499(n) - A401497(n) + 1, n > 1, by Euler's formula.
%F A401498 a(n) = n^2 - n for 1 <= n <= 15.
%Y A401498 Cf. A401497 (regions), A401499 (edges), A371373, A385160, A374338.
%K A401498 nonn,new
%O A401498 1,2
